  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-003",
   "finding": "report #2; report #3; symbol scheme (N_s per point)",
   "old": "- $N_s$ is the number of detected signal photons averaged into one range estimate.",
   "new": "- $N_s$ is the number of detected signal photons averaged into one range estimate (one point), summed over all the pulses fired at that point.\n- $\\Delta R$ is the two-target range resolution (m), $\\sigma_R$ the rms error of one range estimate (m), and $R_{\\rm amb}$ the unambiguous range (m), with the same meaning in subsection 9. Elsewhere in the report $R_{\\max}$ means the maximum detection range.",
   "why": "States that N_s is per point, summed over its pulses, and defines Delta R, sigma_R and R_amb, which the table uses but the list omitted."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-004",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (tau); follow-up #3, #4, #5; report #3 (PRF_ch); report #34 (Hesai AT128)",
   "old": "| Range from delay | $R=c\\,\\Delta t/2$ | 6.67 ns of round trip per metre; 1.33 µs to 200 m | throughout |\n| Two-target range resolution | $\\Delta R\\approx c\\,\\tau_p/2$ for pulse length $\\tau_p$ | 5 ns → 0.75 m; 1 ns → 15 cm | §C3b |\n| Histogram quantization | $c\\,\\Delta t_{\\rm bin}/2$ | 297 ps bins (Adaps ADS6311) → 4.5 cm | §C5 |\n| Single-target precision | $\\sigma_R\\approx(c/2)\\,\\sigma_t/\\sqrt{N_s}$: timing spread $\\sigma_t$ (pulse, detector jitter, TDC) averaged over $N_s$ detected signal photons | $\\sigma_t=100$ ps and $N_s=20$ → ≈0.3 cm (ideal; amplitude walk and background add) | §C4b |\n| Unambiguous range per channel | $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2\\,\\text{PRF})$ | 750 kHz ↔ 200 m; 300 kHz ↔ 500 m | §C4d |\n| Point rate | $\\dot N=N_{\\rm ch}\\times\\text{PRF}_{\\rm ch}$ (per return) | 128 channels × 12 kHz ≈ 1.5 Mpts/s (AT128) | §C4d |",
   "new": "| Range from delay | $R=c\\,\\tau/2$ | 6.67 ns of round trip per metre; 1.33 µs to 200 m | throughout |\n| Two-target range resolution | $\\Delta R\\approx c\\,\\tau_p/2$ for pulse length $\\tau_p$ | 5 ns → 0.75 m; 1 ns → 15 cm | — |\n| Histogram quantization | $c\\,\\Delta t_{\\rm bin}/2$ | 297 ps bins (Adaps ADS6311 [P]) → 4.5 cm; 3 GHz equivalent-time sampling (Sony IMX479 [P]), i.e. 333 ps bins → 5.0 cm | §C5a; the 297 ps example is from §C5 |\n| Single-target precision | $\\sigma_R\\approx(c/2)\\,\\sigma_t/\\sqrt{N_s}$: timing spread $\\sigma_t$ (pulse, detector jitter, TDC) averaged over $N_s$ detected signal photons | $\\sigma_t=100$ ps and $N_s=20$ → ≈0.3 cm (ideal; amplitude walk and background add) | subsection 9; measured precisions in §C5a |\n| Unambiguous range per channel | $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2\\,\\text{PRF}_{\\rm ch})$ | 750 kHz ↔ 200 m; 300 kHz ↔ 500 m | §C4d |\n| Point rate | $\\dot N=N_{\\rm ch}\\times\\text{PRF}_{\\rm ch}$ (per return) | 128 channels × 12 kHz ≈ 1.5 Mpts/s (Hesai AT128) | §C4d |",
   "why": "Delay symbol tau; Used-in pointers made true (no later section applies c tau_p/2; precision is measured in §C5a; §C5a applies the bin relation); PRF_ch per the symbol list; maker named."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-005",
   "finding": "follow-up #3",
   "old": "The precision row divides by $\\sqrt{N_s}$ because averaging $N_s$ independent photon arrival times reduces their scatter by that factor.",
   "new": "The precision row divides by $\\sqrt{N_s}$ because averaging $N_s$ independent photon arrival times reduces their scatter by that factor. The gain assumes that every detected photon is time-stamped independently. A macro-pixel of 3×3 single-photon avalanche diodes (SPADs, subsection 6), read out as one pixel, registers at most nine photons per shot, and only ≈8 of 20 (§C4b, Eq. (64)), so $N_s=20$ must be accumulated over several shots.",
   "why": "The sqrt(N_s) gain needs independently time-stamped photons; a 3x3 macro-pixel fires only about 8 of 9 cells for 20 photons (§C4b Eq. (64))."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-006",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (tau)",
   "old": "inverting $R=c\\,\\Delta t/2$ gives $\\Delta t=2R/c$.",
   "new": "inverting $R=c\\,\\tau/2$ gives $\\tau=2R/c$.",
   "why": "Delay symbol tau, as in the table."
  },
