  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-009",
   "finding": "report #40",
   "old": "**Link budget for an extended Lambertian target**",
   "new": "**Photon budget for an extended Lambertian target**",
   "why": "Lines 1510 and 1899 call this the photon budget; Link budget is subsection 10's title."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-010",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (N_{s,1} per pulse in Eq. (23), E_p for one pulse, E_pt per point); report #2",
   "old": "$$N_s=\\frac{E_p}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\,\\rho\\,\\frac{A_{\\rm rx}}{\\pi R^2}\\,\\eta_{\\rm opt}\\,\\text{PDE}\\,T_{\\rm atm}^2 \\tag{23}$$\n\nwhere:\n- $N_s$ is the mean number of signal photons detected per pulse (dimensionless);\n- $E_p$ is the optical energy of one transmitted pulse (J);\n- $E_{\\rm ph}=h\\nu=hc/\\lambda$ is the energy of one photon (J), with $h=6.63\\times10^{-34}$ J s Planck's constant, $\\nu$ the optical frequency and $\\lambda$ the wavelength, so $E_p/E_{\\rm ph}$ is the number of photons in the pulse;",
   "new": "$$N_{s,1}=\\frac{E_{\\rm p}}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\,\\rho\\,\\frac{A_{\\rm rx}}{\\pi R^2}\\,\\eta_{\\rm opt}\\,\\text{PDE}\\,T_{\\rm atm}^2 \\tag{23}$$\n\nwhere:\n- $N_{s,1}$ is the mean number of signal photons detected per pulse (dimensionless). Summed over the $N_{\\rm pulse}$ pulses fired at one point, it gives the $N_s=N_{\\rm pulse}N_{s,1}$ of the precision row; equivalently, Eq. (23) with the energy per point $E_{\\rm pt}=N_{\\rm pulse}E_{\\rm p}$ in place of $E_{\\rm p}$ gives $N_s$ directly (§C6, Eq. (80));\n- $E_{\\rm p}$ is the optical energy of one transmitted pulse (J);\n- $E_{\\rm ph}=h\\nu=hc/\\lambda$ is the energy of one photon (J), with $h=6.63\\times10^{-34}$ J s Planck's constant, $\\nu$ the optical frequency and $\\lambda$ the wavelength, so $E_{\\rm p}/E_{\\rm ph}$ is the number of photons in the pulse;",
   "why": "Eq. (23) counts one pulse, so its result is N_{s,1}; the link to the per-point N_s and to §C6 Eq. (80) is stated."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-011",
   "finding": "report #40; report #23; symbol scheme (N_{s,1}, E_p); consistency flag (Eq. (23) restates Part A Eq. (5))",
   "old": "Eq. (23) assumes a Lambertian target at normal incidence that fills the beam. Glossy paint or a retroreflector is not Lambertian and can return far more or far less toward the sensor, depending on angle.\n- **Example:** $E_p=100$ nJ at 905 nm ($4.6\\times10^{11}$ photons, of the order of the Class 1 single-pulse limit, §C4c), $\\rho=0.1$, a 25 mm aperture ($A_{\\rm rx}=4.9$ cm²), $R=200$ m, $\\eta_{\\rm opt}=0.5$ and PDE 0.25 give **≈22 photons** before atmospheric loss. Several pulses are usually accumulated per point.\n  - *Substitution.* One 905 nm photon carries $E_{\\rm ph}=h\\nu=hc/\\lambda=6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3.0\\times10^8/(905\\times10^{-9})=2.20\\times10^{-19}$ J, so 100 nJ is $10^{-7}/(2.20\\times10^{-19})=4.6\\times10^{11}$ photons. A 25 mm aperture has $A_{\\rm rx}=\\pi\\times(1.25\\ {\\rm cm})^2=4.9$ cm², so the collected fraction of the Lambertian return is $A_{\\rm rx}/(\\pi R^2)=4.9\\times10^{-4}/(\\pi\\times200^2)=3.9\\times10^{-9}$. Then $N_s=4.6\\times10^{11}\\times0.1\\times3.9\\times10^{-9}\\times0.5\\times0.25\\approx22$ photons.",
   "new": "Eq. (23) assumes a Lambertian target at normal incidence that fills the beam, and a receiver whose field of view covers the whole lit spot. It is Part A's Eq. (5) with the constants written out and the energy counted in photons. Glossy paint or a retroreflector is not Lambertian and can return far more or far less toward the sensor, depending on angle.\n- **Example:** $E_{\\rm p}=100$ nJ at 905 nm ($4.6\\times10^{11}$ photons; between the 905 nm Class 1 limits of 198 nJ for an isolated pulse and 79 nJ per pulse in long trains, subsection 5 and §C4c), $\\rho=0.1$, a 25 mm aperture ($A_{\\rm rx}=4.9$ cm²), $R=200$ m, $\\eta_{\\rm opt}=0.5$ and PDE 0.25 give **$N_{s,1}\\approx22$ photons** per pulse before atmospheric loss. Several pulses are usually accumulated per point.\n  - *Substitution.* One 905 nm photon carries $E_{\\rm ph}=h\\nu=hc/\\lambda=6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3.0\\times10^8/(905\\times10^{-9})=2.20\\times10^{-19}$ J, so 100 nJ is $10^{-7}/(2.20\\times10^{-19})=4.6\\times10^{11}$ photons. A 25 mm aperture has $A_{\\rm rx}=\\pi\\times(1.25\\ {\\rm cm})^2=4.9$ cm², so the collected fraction of the Lambertian return is $A_{\\rm rx}/(\\pi R^2)=4.9\\times10^{-4}/(\\pi\\times200^2)=3.9\\times10^{-9}$. Then $N_{s,1}=4.6\\times10^{11}\\times0.1\\times3.9\\times10^{-9}\\times0.5\\times0.25\\approx22$ photons.",
   "why": "States the field-of-view assumption, says which Class 1 limits bracket 100 nJ, and points to Part A Eq. (5)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-012",
   "finding": "report #22; report #12 (first §C10.3a mention); follow-up #2; symbol scheme (E_p)",
   "old": "- **Scaling.** Signal-limited range goes as $R\\propto\\sqrt{E_pA_{\\rm rx}\\rho}$, because holding $N_s\\propto E_pA_{\\rm rx}\\rho/R^2$ fixed makes $R^2$ proportional to $E_pA_{\\rm rx}\\rho$. When background noise dominates and also grows with $A_{\\rm rx}$ ($N_b\\propto A_{\\rm rx}$, where $N_b$ is the mean number of background counts, mostly from sunlight, in the bins that hold the echo), the signal-to-noise ratio is SNR $\\approx N_s/\\sqrt{N_b}\\propto(A_{\\rm rx}/R^2)/\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}=\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}/R^2$. Holding the SNR fixed then gives $R^2\\propto\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}$, i.e. $R\\propto A_{\\rm rx}^{1/4}$ (§C4d, §C10.3a). In daylight, doubling the lens diameter therefore buys far less range than it does at night.\n- **Small targets.** A target smaller than the beam (debris, a tyre) intercepts a fraction of it that falls as $1/R^2$, so its return falls as $1/R^4$ (§E12).\n- **Photon-counting SNR** in the return's histogram bins is $\\text{SNR}\\approx N_s/\\sqrt{N_s+N_b}$, with $N_s$ the signal counts and $N_b$ the background counts in the same bins. Both are Poisson-distributed, so the noise is the square root of the total count. Sunlight sets $N_b$ (§C4c, §C6). Dead time (the interval after each detection during which a photon-counting pixel is blind) and pile-up (the resulting bias toward early photons) distort the histogram at high count rates (§C4b, §C6).",
   "new": "- **Photon-counting SNR** in the return's histogram bins is $\\text{SNR}\\approx N_s/\\sqrt{N_s+N_b}$, with $N_s$ the signal counts and $N_b$ the background counts in the same bins, both summed over the pulses of one point. Both are Poisson-distributed, so the noise is the square root of the total count. Sunlight sets $N_b$ (§C4c, §C6). Dead time (the interval after each detection during which a photon-counting pixel is blind) and pile-up (the resulting bias toward early photons) distort the histogram at high count rates (§C4b, §C6).\n- **Scaling.** Signal-limited range goes as $R\\propto\\sqrt{E_{\\rm p}A_{\\rm rx}\\rho}$, because holding $N_s\\propto E_{\\rm p}A_{\\rm rx}\\rho/R^2$ fixed (at a fixed number of pulses per point) makes $R^2$ proportional to $E_{\\rm p}A_{\\rm rx}\\rho$. When background noise dominates ($N_b\\gg N_s$) and also grows with $A_{\\rm rx}$ ($N_b\\propto A_{\\rm rx}$), the SNR above becomes $N_s/\\sqrt{N_b}\\propto(A_{\\rm rx}/R^2)/\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}=\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}/R^2$. Holding the SNR fixed then gives $R^2\\propto\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}$, i.e. $R\\propto A_{\\rm rx}^{1/4}$ (§C4d, and §C10.3a, which contrasts one spatial mode with an integrating pixel). In daylight, doubling the lens diameter (4× the area) therefore buys a factor $4^{1/4}=1.41$ in range instead of 2 at night. When the sunlit target itself supplies the background, $N_b\\propto\\rho$ too, and range scales as $\\rho^{1/4}$ rather than $\\rho^{1/2}$ (§C0 subsection 10.8).\n- **Small targets.** A target smaller than the beam (debris, a tyre) intercepts a fraction of it that falls as $1/R^2$, so its return falls as $1/R^4$ (§E11.3, Eq. (169)).",
   "why": "Defines the photon-counting SNR before Scaling uses it, quantifies the daylight aperture gain, adds the sunlit-target rho^(1/4) case and points the 1/R^4 law to its derivation (§E11.3 Eq. (169))."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-013",
   "finding": "report #26",
   "old": "in a chirp time $T$ (s). The sweep speed is the chirp rate $\\kappa=B/T$, in Hz/s.",
   "new": "in one ramp of duration $T$ (s); a triangular chirp is a rising (up) ramp followed by a falling (down) ramp. The sweep speed is the chirp rate $\\kappa=B/T$, in Hz/s.",
   "why": "Names the up- and down-ramps and their common duration T once, so ramp is used throughout."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-014",
   "finding": "report #2; report #26; symbol scheme (N_mode with n-bar = eta N_mode)",
   "old": "- $f_{\\rm up}$, $f_{\\rm down}$: the measured beat frequencies during the rising (up) and falling (down) segments of a triangular chirp, which last $T_{\\rm up}$ and $T_{\\rm down}$;\n- $\\Delta R$: range resolution (the smallest separation at which two targets appear as two peaks); $\\sigma_R$: range precision (rms error for one target); $\\Delta v$: velocity resolution;\n- SNR: the signal-to-noise ratio of the beat tone after the Fourier transform (FFT, fast Fourier transform) over one chirp;\n- $\\Delta\\nu$: the laser's linewidth (Hz), the full width at half maximum of its optical spectrum, taken as Lorentzian; $L_c$: the resulting coherence length (m), the path difference over which the LO and the echo stay phase-correlated;\n- $\\varepsilon$: the fractional error in the chirp rate;\n- $T_{\\rm dwell}$: the time spent per point, and $N_{\\rm ch}$ the number of parallel coherent channels;\n- $N_s$: the number of signal photons in the LO's spatial mode during the chirp, and $\\eta$ the overall detection efficiency, the fraction of those photons converted into signal photoelectrons.",
   "new": "- $f_{\\rm up}$, $f_{\\rm down}$: the measured beat frequencies during the up- and down-ramps, each of duration $T$;\n- $\\Delta R$: range resolution (the smallest separation at which two targets appear as two peaks); $\\sigma_R$: range precision (rms error for one target); $\\Delta v$: velocity resolution;\n- SNR: the signal-to-noise ratio of the beat tone after the Fourier transform (FFT, fast Fourier transform) over one ramp;\n- $\\Delta\\nu$: the laser's linewidth (Hz), the full width at half maximum of its optical spectrum, taken as Lorentzian; $L_c$: the resulting coherence length (m), the path difference over which the LO and the echo stay phase-correlated;\n- $\\varepsilon$: the fractional error in the chirp rate;\n- $T_{\\rm dwell}$: the time spent per point (s), $2T$ for one up- and one down-ramp, and $N_{\\rm ch}$ the number of parallel coherent channels. Only the last $T-\\tau$ of each ramp yields a clean beat, because the echo arrives $\\tau$ after the ramp starts; §C7b subsection 2(g) writes the same dwell as $T_{\\rm dwell}=2(T_u+\\tau)$, with $T_u=T-\\tau$ the useful part of each ramp;\n- $N_{\\rm mode}$: the number of signal photons that arrive in the LO's spatial mode and polarization during one ramp, and $\\eta$ the overall detection efficiency, the fraction of those photons converted into signal photoelectrons. Then $\\bar n=\\eta N_{\\rm mode}$ is the number of signal photoelectrons; subsection 10 uses $\\bar n$ for the total over both ramps of a point.",
   "why": "T_up = T_down = T made explicit, T_dwell given a unit and the round-trip caveat of §C7b, and N_s renamed N_mode with the link to n-bar."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-015",
   "finding": "report #26; report #35; report #6",
   "old": "| Range resolution | $\\Delta R=c/(2B)$. It is set by the sweep bandwidth, not the chirp duration: the FFT bin $1/T$ maps to $c/2B$ | 1 GHz → 15 cm; 4 GHz → 3.75 cm | §C7 |\n| Range precision | $\\sigma_R\\approx\\Delta R/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}$, within a factor of order one (frequency estimation of a tone in noise) | 15 cm resolution at 20 dB SNR → ≈1 cm | §C7a |",
   "new": "| Range resolution | $\\Delta R=c/(2B)$. It is set by the sweep bandwidth, not the ramp duration: the FFT bin $1/T$ maps to $c/(2B)$ | 1 GHz → 15 cm; 4 GHz → 3.75 cm | §C7 |\n| Range precision | $\\sigma_R\\approx\\Delta R/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}$, within a factor of order one (frequency estimation of a tone in noise) | 15 cm resolution at 20 dB SNR → 1.5 cm by this relation; 0.6–0.8 cm at the Cramér–Rao bound | §C7a |",
   "why": "Ramp wording; c/(2B) bracketed; the precision cell gives what the relation yields (1.5 cm) and the Cramer-Rao value instead of an unexplained 1 cm."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-016",
   "finding": "report #26; report #35; report #34 (I/Q); report #11",
   "old": "| Separating range and velocity | Up and down chirps give $f_{\\rm up}=f_b-f_D$ and $f_{\\rm down}=f_b+f_D$ (approaching target); then $R=cT(f_{\\rm up}+f_{\\rm down})/(4B)$ and $v_r=\\lambda(f_{\\rm down}-f_{\\rm up})/4$ | An unseparated 10 m/s would shift range by $cf_D/2\\kappa\\approx19$ m | §C7 |\n| Velocity resolution | $\\Delta v\\approx\\lambda/(2T)$ per chirp segment | 10 µs → 7.8 cm/s; 2 µs → 39 cm/s | §C7a |\n| Receiver bandwidth | $f_b+\\lvert f_D\\rvert$ must fit the digitizer: at least that sample rate with complex (I/Q) sampling, twice it with real sampling | 300 m and ±50 m/s → 265 MHz | §C7a |\n| Coherence | Coherent share of the beat power $\\eta_{\\rm coh}=e^{-2\\pi\\Delta\\nu\\cdot2R/c}$; Lorentzian coherence length $L_c=c/(\\pi\\Delta\\nu)$ | 100 kHz: $L_c\\approx950$ m, yet only 28% of the beat stays coherent at 300 m (43% at 200 m) | §C7, §C7b |",
   "new": "| Separating range and velocity | Up and down ramps give $f_{\\rm up}=f_b-f_D$ and $f_{\\rm down}=f_b+f_D$ (approaching target); then $R=cT(f_{\\rm up}+f_{\\rm down})/(4B)$ and $v_r=\\lambda(f_{\\rm down}-f_{\\rm up})/4$ | An unseparated 10 m/s would shift range by $c f_D/(2\\kappa)\\approx19$ m | §C7 |\n| Velocity resolution | $\\Delta v\\approx\\lambda/(2T)$ per ramp | 10 µs → 7.8 cm/s; 2 µs → 39 cm/s | §C7a |\n| Receiver bandwidth | $f_b+\\lvert f_D\\rvert$ must fit the digitizer: at least that sample rate with complex in-phase/quadrature (I/Q) sampling, twice it with real sampling | 300 m and ±50 m/s → 265 MHz | §C7a |\n| Coherence | Coherent share of the beat power $\\eta_{\\rm coh}=e^{-2\\pi\\Delta\\nu\\cdot2R/c}$; Lorentzian coherence length $L_c=c/(\\pi\\Delta\\nu)$ | 100 kHz: $L_c\\approx950$ m, but $\\eta_{\\rm coh}=e^{-4R/L_c}$ is only 28% at 300 m (43% at 200 m) | §C7, §C7b |",
   "why": "Ramp wording, bracketed fraction, I/Q expanded, and the coherence cell states the relation that links L_c to the coherent share."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-017",
   "finding": "report #26; report #2; report #7; report #12",
   "old": "| Dwell and point rate | One point per channel per $\\approx T_{\\rm up}+T_{\\rm down}$, so $\\dot N\\approx N_{\\rm ch}/T_{\\rm dwell}$ | 20 µs dwell → 50 kpts/s per channel | §C7, §C7a |\n| Shot-noise-limited SNR | With a strong LO, $\\text{SNR}\\approx\\eta N_s$, where $N_s$ counts signal photons in the LO's spatial mode during $T$ | A few photons suffice in principle; speckle fading, the search over thousands of bins and a peak needed in both chirp ramps raise the practical need to ≈90 (four looks) to ≈260 (one look) per point (subsection 10.3) | §C7, §C10.3a |",
   "new": "| Dwell and point rate | One point per channel per up–down ramp pair, $T_{\\rm dwell}=2T$, so $\\dot N\\approx N_{\\rm ch}/T_{\\rm dwell}$ | 20 µs dwell → 50 kpts/s per channel | §C7, §C7a |\n| Shot-noise-limited SNR | With a strong LO, the SNR of one ramp is $\\text{SNR}\\approx\\bar n=\\eta N_{\\rm mode}$, the signal photoelectrons collected in that ramp | A few signal photoelectrons per ramp suffice in principle. Speckle fading, the search over thousands of bins and a peak needed in both ramps raise the practical need to ≈260 photoelectrons per point with one look and ≈90 with four looks, counted over both ramps and all looks (§C0 subsection 10.3) | §C7, §C10.3a |",
   "why": "Dwell written as 2T; the SNR row uses N_mode and n-bar, and the 90/260 figures are labelled as photoelectrons per point over both ramps and all looks, with the §C0 prefix."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-018",
   "finding": "consistency flag (chirp parameters differ from §C7a and §C0 subsection 10.3)",
   "old": "- *How the numbers are derived* [E], for 1,550 nm, $B=1$ GHz and $T=10$ µs, so that $\\kappa=B/T=10^{14}$ Hz/s:",
   "new": "- *How the numbers are derived* [E], for 1,550 nm, $B=1$ GHz and $T=10$ µs, so that $\\kappa=B/T=10^{14}$ Hz/s. These are illustrative values, as in §C7 Eq. (85); the §C7a model and §C0 subsection 10.3 use 5 µs ramps:",
   "why": "Says that the 1 GHz / 10 us values are illustrative and that the model uses 5 us ramps."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-019",
   "finding": "report #6",
   "old": "  - **Range precision:** 20 dB is an SNR of 100, so $\\Delta R/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}=15\\ {\\rm cm}/10=1.5$ cm. The Cramér–Rao bound for the frequency of a tone, $\\sigma_f=a/(T\\sqrt{\\rm SNR})$, has a prefactor $a$ between $\\sqrt6/(2\\pi)\\approx0.39$ (complex I/Q samples) and $\\sqrt{12}/(2\\pi)\\approx0.55$ (real samples), which gives 0.6–0.8 cm. The table's ≈1 cm lies within this 0.6–1.5 cm span.",
   "new": "  - **Range precision:** 20 dB is an SNR of 100, so $\\Delta R/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}=15\\ {\\rm cm}/10=1.5$ cm. The Cramér–Rao bound, the smallest rms error any unbiased estimator can reach, gives for the frequency of a tone $\\sigma_f=a/(T\\sqrt{\\rm SNR})$, with $a=\\sqrt6/(2\\pi)\\approx0.39$ for complex I/Q samples or $\\sqrt{12}/(2\\pi)\\approx0.55$ for real samples. Since $R=c\\,f_b/(2\\kappa)$, a beat-frequency error $\\sigma_f$ is a range error $\\sigma_R=c\\,\\sigma_f/(2\\kappa)=a\\,c/(2\\kappa T\\sqrt{\\rm SNR})=a\\,\\Delta R/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}$, using $\\kappa T=B$ and $\\Delta R=c/(2B)$. That is $0.39\\times1.5=0.59$ cm to $0.55\\times1.5=0.83$ cm, i.e. 0.6–0.8 cm.",
   "why": "Explains the Cramer-Rao bound and adds the skipped step from the frequency bound to the range error, with substitution."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-020",
   "finding": "report #26",
   "old": "for a 10 µs segment",
   "new": "for a 10 µs ramp",
   "why": "Ramp wording."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-021",
   "finding": "report #11",
   "old": "  - **Coherence length:** $L_c=c/(\\pi\\Delta\\nu)=3.0\\times10^8/(\\pi\\times10^5)\\approx955$ m for a 100 kHz linewidth, shown as ≈950 m. **Coherent share:** $2\\pi\\times10^{5}\\times2.0\\times10^{-6}=1.26$, so $e^{-1.26}=0.28$ at 300 m; $2\\pi\\times10^{5}\\times1.33\\times10^{-6}=0.84$, so $e^{-0.84}=0.43$ at 200 m (§C7b subsection 2(e)).",
   "new": "  - **Coherence length:** $L_c=c/(\\pi\\Delta\\nu)=3.0\\times10^8/(\\pi\\times10^5)\\approx955$ m for a 100 kHz linewidth, shown as ≈950 m. **Coherent share:** $2\\pi\\times10^{5}\\times2.0\\times10^{-6}=1.26$, so $e^{-1.26}=0.28$ at 300 m; $2\\pi\\times10^{5}\\times1.33\\times10^{-6}=0.84$, so $e^{-0.84}=0.43$ at 200 m. Here $2.0\\times10^{-6}$ s and $1.33\\times10^{-6}$ s are the round-trip delays $\\tau=2R/c$ at 300 and 200 m. The two relations are linked: since $\\pi\\Delta\\nu=c/L_c$, $\\eta_{\\rm coh}=e^{-2\\pi\\Delta\\nu(2R/c)}=e^{-4R/L_c}$, so the coherent share falls to $1/e$ already at $R=L_c/4\\approx240$ m, and the rule of thumb $L_c\\ge2R$ keeps only $e^{-2}=14\\%$ at its limit (§C7b subsection 2(e)).",
   "why": "Names the delays and states the link e^(-4R/L_c), so the 28% at L_c = 950 m is no longer a puzzle."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-022",
   "finding": "report #26; report #35 (pts/s)",
   "old": "  - **Dwell:** one point per 20 µs is $1/(20\\times10^{-6}\\ {\\rm s})=5\\times10^4$ points/s per channel.",
   "new": "  - **Dwell:** $T_{\\rm dwell}=2T=2\\times10\\ \\mu{\\rm s}=20\\ \\mu{\\rm s}$, so one channel delivers $1/(20\\times10^{-6}\\ {\\rm s})=5\\times10^4$ pts/s.",
   "why": "Dwell derived from T, with substitution; unit standardized."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-023",
   "finding": "report #25; symbol scheme (scan rate theta-dot, Delta f_scan)",
   "old": "- **Mechanical scanning adds two coherent-specific errors** (§C7, §C10.3a, §C4d). Here $\\Omega$ is the optical angular scan rate in rad/s (a rate, not a solid angle) and $D$ the beam diameter on the scanner.\n  - *Walk-off*, $\\Delta\\theta=2R\\Omega/c$: during the round trip $2R/c$ the scanner turns, so the echo comes back at an angle $\\Delta\\theta$ away from the LO's direction.\n  - *Scanner-induced Doppler*, $\\Delta f\\approx\\Omega D/\\lambda$: the moving mirror spreads the return's optical frequency by about $\\Delta f$.",
   "new": "- **Mechanical scanning adds two coherent-specific errors** (§C7, §C10.3a, §C4d). Here $\\dot\\theta$ is the optical scan rate, the angular speed of the beam (rad/s), and $D$ the beam diameter on the scanner.\n  - *Walk-off*, $\\theta_{\\rm wo}=2R\\dot\\theta/c$: during the round trip $\\tau=2R/c$ the beam direction moves on, so the echo comes back at an angle $\\theta_{\\rm wo}$ away from the LO's direction. For example [E], a polygon sweeping 45,600°/s (796 rad/s) gives $\\theta_{\\rm wo}=2\\times200\\times796/(3.0\\times10^8)=1.06\\times10^{-3}$ rad (0.061°) at 200 m (§C4d, Eq. (71)), about 3.4 times the divergence $\\lambda/D=0.31$ mrad of a 5 mm beam at 1,550 nm.\n  - *Scanner-induced Doppler*, $\\Delta f_{\\rm scan}\\approx\\dot\\theta D/\\lambda$ (Eq. (86) of §C7): the moving mirror spreads the return's optical frequency by about $\\Delta f_{\\rm scan}$. The same polygon with a 5 mm beam gives $796\\times5\\times10^{-3}/(1.55\\times10^{-6})=2.6$ MHz, about 26 FFT bins of a 10 µs ramp (§C7).",
   "why": "Scan rate no longer shares Omega with solid angle, the spread no longer shares Delta f with the bandwidth of Eq. (26), walk-off no longer shares Delta theta with point spacing, and both errors get a number."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-024",
   "finding": "follow-up #8; follow-up #9; symbol scheme (delta theta for point spacing); report #39 (N_par)",
   "old": "- $R\\,\\theta$ is the width of the footprint, the patch a beam or pixel of angular width $\\theta$ covers at range $R$.\n- $\\Theta_h$, $\\Theta_v$ are the horizontal and vertical fields of view, and $\\Delta\\theta_h$, $\\Delta\\theta_v$ the angular spacing of points along each.\n- $S$ is the mechanical sweep rate: the number of times per second the scanner must sweep the beam across the field.\n- $N_{\\rm lines}$ is the number of scan lines per frame, $f_{\\rm frame}$ the frame rate (frames per second), and $N_{\\rm par}$ the number of lines read in parallel (parallel channels).",
   "new": "- $R\\,\\theta$ is the width of the footprint, the patch a beam or pixel of angular width $\\theta$ covers at range $R$; likewise $R\\,\\delta\\theta$ is the spacing at range $R$ of points an angular step $\\delta\\theta$ apart.\n- $\\Theta_H$, $\\Theta_V$ are the horizontal and vertical fields of view, and $\\delta\\theta_H$, $\\delta\\theta_V$ the angular spacing of points along each (degrees), as in §C3b Eq. (61).\n- $S$ is the mechanical sweep rate: the number of times per second the scanner must sweep the beam across the field.\n- $N_{\\rm lines}$ is the number of scan lines per frame, $f_{\\rm frame}$ the frame rate (frames per second), and $N_{\\rm par}$ the number of lines read in parallel (parallel channels; $N_{\\rm par}=N_{\\rm ch}$ of subsection 1 when each channel draws its own line).",
   "why": "Adopts the delta-theta notation of §C3b Eq. (61) with units, extends the footprint symbol to point spacing, and ties N_par to N_ch."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-025",
   "finding": "follow-up #9; follow-up #10; follow-up #8; report #17; report #39",
   "old": "| Footprint at range | $R\\,\\theta$ | 0.1° at 200 m → 35 cm; 0.05° → 17 cm | §E12 |\n| Points per frame | $(\\Theta_h/\\Delta\\theta_h)(\\Theta_v/\\Delta\\theta_v)$ | 120°/0.1° × 25°/0.2° = 150,000; at 10 Hz, 1.5 Mpts/s | §C3b |\n| Mechanical sweep rate | $S=N_{\\rm lines}f_{\\rm frame}/N_{\\rm par}$; a bidirectional resonant mirror needs $f_{\\rm mirror}=S/2$ | 128 lines at 10 Hz: 10 sweeps/s with 128 channels, 1,280 with one | §C4d |\n| Étendue (optical invariant) | $A\\,\\Omega$ is conserved through passive optics. At a fixed f-number, a smaller pixel pitch at the same pixel angle therefore means a smaller aperture, while a smaller pixel angle at the same pitch means a larger one | §C6 worked example | §C6, §C10.3a |",
   "new": "| Footprint or point spacing at range | $R\\,\\theta$ for a beam or pixel of width $\\theta$; $R\\,\\delta\\theta$ for points $\\delta\\theta$ apart | 0.1° at 200 m → 35 cm; 0.05° → 17 cm | §E11.3, §E12 (Eqs. (179), (180)) |\n| Points per frame | $(\\Theta_H/\\delta\\theta_H)(\\Theta_V/\\delta\\theta_V)$; times $f_{\\rm frame}$ this is the point rate, Eq. (61) | 1,200 × 128 = 153,600 (the Hesai AT128 grid, 0.1° × ≈0.2° over 120° × 25.4°, §C3b); at 10 Hz, 1.536 Mpts/s, the point rate of subsection 1 | §C3b (Eq. (61)) |\n| Mechanical sweep rate | $S=N_{\\rm lines}f_{\\rm frame}/N_{\\rm par}$; a bidirectional resonant mirror needs $f_{\\rm mirror}=S/2$ | 128 lines at 10 Hz: 10 sweeps/s with 128 channels, 1,280 with one | §C4d (Eq. (69)) |\n| Étendue (optical invariant) | $A\\,\\Omega$ is conserved through passive optics. With $\\theta_{\\rm pix}=p/f$ and $D=f/F_\\#$, a pixel fixes $D\\,\\theta_{\\rm pix}=p/F_\\#$. At a fixed f-number a smaller pitch at the same pixel angle therefore means a smaller aperture, and a smaller pixel angle at the same pitch a larger one | $p/F_\\#=30\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/1.2=25\\ \\mu{\\rm m}$; at 0.88 mrad, $D=25\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/0.88\\ {\\rm mrad}=28$ mm | §C6, §C10.3a |",
   "why": "Footprint row covers point spacing as §E11.3 and §E12 use it; points-per-frame row uses the real AT128 grid and Eq. (61); the etendue row shows the D theta = p/F# step with numbers."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-026",
   "finding": "follow-up #10",
   "old": "- **Points per frame:** $(120^\\circ/0.1^\\circ)\\times(25^\\circ/0.2^\\circ)=1{,}200\\times125=150{,}000$; at 10 frames/s that is $1.5\\times10^6$ pts/s.",
   "new": "- **Points per frame:** for the Hesai AT128 grid, $\\delta\\theta_V=25.4^\\circ/128=0.198^\\circ$, so $(\\Theta_H/\\delta\\theta_H)(\\Theta_V/\\delta\\theta_V)=(120^\\circ/0.1^\\circ)\\times(25.4^\\circ/0.198^\\circ)=1{,}200\\times128=153{,}600$; at 10 frames/s that is $1.536\\times10^6$ pts/s.",
   "why": "Derivation matches the AT128 grid used in the table and in subsection 1."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-027",
   "finding": "report #38",
   "old": "- **Sweep rate:** $S=N_{\\rm lines}f_{\\rm frame}/N_{\\rm par}=128\\times10\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}/128=10$ sweeps/s with 128 parallel channels, and $128\\times10/1=1{,}280$ with one. A bidirectional resonant mirror would then run at $f_{\\rm mirror}=S/2$, i.e. 5 Hz or 640 Hz.",
   "new": "- **Sweep rate:** $S=N_{\\rm lines}f_{\\rm frame}/N_{\\rm par}=128\\times10\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}/128=10$ sweeps/s with 128 parallel channels, and $128\\times10/1=1{,}280$ with one. With 128 channels, 10 sweeps/s is one facet per frame of the Hesai AT128's three-facet mirror turning at 200 rpm (§C4d); with one channel, a bidirectional resonant mirror would run at $f_{\\rm mirror}=S/2=1{,}280/2=640$ Hz.",
   "why": "No resonant mirror runs at 5 Hz; the AT128 uses one facet of a three-facet mirror per frame (§C4d)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-028",
   "finding": "report #29",
   "old": "The practical consequence is that parallel channels relieve the scanner: with 128 channels a slow, robust mirror suffices, while a single beam needs a mirror more than a hundred times faster (§C4d).",
   "new": "The practical consequence is that parallel channels relieve the scanner: with 128 channels a slow, robust mirror suffices, while a single beam needs a mirror more than a hundred times faster (§C4d). Taken together, the relations of this subsection set the trade-off named at its start. Halving the point spacing on both axes quadruples the points per frame, so at a fixed point rate it quarters the frame rate or needs four times the channels. At a fixed average laser power it also leaves a quarter of the energy per point, which halves the signal-limited range, since that range scales as the square root of the energy per point (subsection 1). At a fixed pitch and f-number, a finer pixel angle also needs a longer focal length and so a larger aperture, which means larger optics and, for the same field, a larger sensor (§C6).",
   "why": "States the resolution-range-scan-speed trade-off that the subsection's opening promises."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-029",
   "finding": "report #13",
   "old": "- **FMCW** measures each point's radial velocity from its Doppler shift, with the resolution in subsection 2.\n- **ToF** infers velocity by tracking across frames: $\\sigma_v\\approx\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma_R/\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}$. Here $\\sigma_v$ is the rms velocity error, $\\sigma_R$ the range precision of one measurement and $\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}=1/f_{\\rm frame}$ the time between frames. The factor $\\sqrt2$ appears because the velocity is the difference of two independent range measurements, each with error $\\sigma_R$. At 3 cm precision and 10 Hz ($\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}=0.1$ s) that is $\\sqrt2\\times0.03\\ {\\rm m}/0.1\\ {\\rm s}\\approx0.42$ m/s, i.e. ≈0.4 m/s from two frames, and it needs points to be associated between frames. Tracking over more frames lowers the noise but adds latency.",
   "new": "- **FMCW** measures each point's radial velocity from its Doppler shift in one look. Its resolution is $\\Delta v=\\lambda/(2T)=7.8$ cm/s for a 10 µs ramp (subsection 2), and its precision is finer, of order $\\Delta v/\\sqrt{\\rm SNR}=7.8/10\\approx0.8$ cm/s at 20 dB SNR [E].\n- **ToF** infers velocity by tracking across frames: $\\sigma_v\\approx\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma_R/\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}$. Here $\\sigma_v$ is the rms velocity error, $\\sigma_R$ the range precision of one measurement and $\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}=1/f_{\\rm frame}$ the time between frames. The factor $\\sqrt2$ appears because the velocity is the difference of two independent range measurements, each with error $\\sigma_R$. Take an assumed range precision of 3 cm [E], ten times the ideal 0.3 cm of subsection 1 to allow for amplitude walk and background, and comparable with the 2–4 cm the Sony AS-DT1 measures outdoors at 10 m (§C5a). At 10 Hz ($\\Delta t_{\\rm frame}=0.1$ s) that gives $\\sqrt2\\times0.03\\ {\\rm m}/0.1\\ {\\rm s}\\approx0.42$ m/s, i.e. ≈0.4 m/s from two frames, about fifty times FMCW's single-look precision. It also needs points to be associated between frames. Tracking over more frames lowers the noise but adds latency.",
   "why": "Compares precision with precision (FMCW 0.8 cm/s against ToF 0.42 m/s) and tags and explains the assumed 3 cm."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-030",
   "finding": "report #34 (IEC); report #36 (Class 1 limits untagged)",
   "old": "- **Class 1 (IEC 60825-1:2014).** Class 1 is the least hazardous class of the laser-safety standard, defined by limits on the emission accessible under the standard's measurement conditions; automotive lidars are designed to it. At 905/940 nm the limits are ≈198/233 nJ per isolated small-source pulse and ≈79/93 nJ per pulse in long trains. At 1,550 nm the long-exposure limit is ≈10 mW, against ≈1 mW at 905 nm, because the front of the eye absorbs 1,550 nm light before it can be focused onto the retina. The classification must hold under reasonably foreseeable single faults, including a stalled scanner (§C4d).",
   "new": "- **Class 1 (IEC 60825-1:2014).** IEC 60825-1 is the laser-safety standard of the International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), and Class 1 is its least hazardous class, defined by limits on the emission accessible under the standard's measurement conditions; automotive lidars are designed to it. At 905/940 nm the limits are ≈198/233 nJ per isolated small-source pulse and ≈79/93 nJ per pulse in long trains [P, IEC 60825-1:2014; §C4c]. At 1,550 nm the long-exposure limit is ≈10 mW, against ≈1 mW at 905 nm [P/E, IEC 60825-1:2014; §C4], because the front of the eye absorbs 1,550 nm light before it can be focused onto the retina. The classification must hold under reasonably foreseeable single faults, including a stalled scanner (§C4d).",
   "why": "Expands IEC and tags the Class 1 limits with the standard and the section that derives them."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-031",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "  $$r_{\\rm bg}\\propto E_\\odot(\\lambda)\\,\\Delta\\lambda\\,(\\rho/\\pi)\\,\\Omega_{\\rm pix}\\,A_{\\rm rx}\\,\\eta_{\\rm opt}\\,\\text{PDE} \\tag{24}$$",
   "new": "  $$r_{\\rm bg}=\\frac{E_\\odot(\\lambda)\\,\\Delta\\lambda}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\,\\frac{\\rho_{\\rm scene}}{\\pi}\\,\\Omega_{\\rm pix}\\,A_{\\rm rx}\\,\\eta_{\\rm opt}\\,\\text{PDE} \\tag{24}$$",
   "why": "The one missing factor (1/E_ph) is named, so Eq. (24) is written as the equality of §C6 Eq. (81), with the scene reflectance rho_scene."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-032",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "  - $\\rho/\\pi$ turns that irradiance into the radiance of a Lambertian scene of reflectance $\\rho$ (per steradian);",
   "new": "  - $E_{\\rm ph}=hc/\\lambda$ is the photon energy (J), which converts the in-band solar power into a photon rate;\n  - $\\rho_{\\rm scene}/\\pi$ turns the solar irradiance into the radiance of a Lambertian scene (per steradian), where $\\rho_{\\rm scene}$ is the reflectance of the sunlit scene in the pixel's view, not the target reflectance $\\rho$ of Eq. (23);",
   "why": "Defines E_ph and rho_scene, and separates the scene reflectance from the target reflectance of Eq. (23)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-033",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "  The proportionality absorbs the conversion from collected power to photon rate, a factor $1/E_{\\rm ph}=\\lambda/(hc)$.",
   "new": "  Eq. (24) is the relation §C6 uses as Eq. (81). *Example* [E, inputs of §C6(d)]: at 905 nm, $E_\\odot\\approx0.75$ W m⁻² nm⁻¹ and $E_{\\rm ph}=2.20\\times10^{-19}$ J, so $E_\\odot/E_{\\rm ph}=3.4\\times10^{18}$ photons s⁻¹ m⁻² nm⁻¹. With a 20 nm filter, $\\rho_{\\rm scene}=0.9$, a 0.1° × 0.05° pixel ($\\Omega_{\\rm pix}=1.52\\times10^{-6}$ sr), a 25 mm aperture ($A_{\\rm rx}=4.91\\times10^{-4}$ m²), $\\eta_{\\rm opt}=0.5$ and PDE 0.3, $r_{\\rm bg}=3.4\\times10^{18}\\times20\\times(0.9/\\pi)\\times1.52\\times10^{-6}\\times4.91\\times10^{-4}\\times0.5\\times0.3\\approx2.2\\times10^9$ counts/s.",
   "why": "Replaces the proportionality remark with the link to §C6 Eq. (81) and a worked number with substitution."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-034",
   "finding": "report #34 (APD, SPAD before expansion)",
   "old": "either by a controlled avalanche (a linear APD) or by a runaway one (a Geiger-mode SPAD).",
   "new": "either by a controlled avalanche (a linear avalanche photodiode, APD) or by a runaway one (a single-photon avalanche diode, SPAD, in Geiger mode).",
   "why": "Expands APD and SPAD at first use."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-035",
   "finding": "report #34 (expansion moved to first use); report #35 (photoelectron)",
   "old": "An avalanche photodiode (APD) is a photodiode biased just below breakdown. Each photo-electron",
   "new": "An APD is a photodiode biased just below breakdown. Each photoelectron",
   "why": "APD is expanded at first use above; spelling standardized."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-036",
   "finding": "report #5; spec (definition of k)",
   "old": "  for electron-initiated multiplication. Here $F(M)$ is the excess-noise factor, the factor by which the gain's randomness raises the shot-noise power above that of a noiseless amplifier with the same mean gain ($F=1$ would be noiseless). $k$ is the ratio of the hole to the electron ionization coefficient, i.e. how readily holes, rather than electrons, trigger further ionization; a small $k$ means an avalanche driven by one carrier type, which is quieter.",
   "new": "  for multiplication started by the more strongly ionizing carrier. Here $F(M)$ is the excess-noise factor, the factor by which the gain's randomness raises the shot-noise power above that of a noiseless amplifier with the same mean gain ($F=1$ would be noiseless). $k$ is the ionization coefficient (ionizations per unit length) of the weaker-ionizing carrier divided by that of the stronger one: holes over electrons in silicon, where electrons start the avalanche, and electrons over holes in the InP multiplication layer of an InGaAs/InP APD, where holes start it. A small $k$ means an avalanche driven by one carrier type, which is quieter.",
   "why": "k is the weaker-ionizing over the stronger-ionizing carrier's coefficient (hole/electron in Si, electron/hole in InP), and Eq. (25) holds when the stronger one starts the avalanche."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-037",
   "finding": "report #15",
   "old": "- **Consequence.** Gain helps until the multiplied shot noise overtakes the noise of the amplifier that follows the detector (a transimpedance amplifier, TIA, which converts current into voltage); beyond that, more gain lowers the SNR. The useful gain therefore ends where $F$ starts to climb. Substituting the $k$ of each material into Eq. (25) gives:",
   "new": "- **Consequence.** Gain helps only until the multiplied shot noise $2qI_{\\rm ph}M^2F(M)\\,\\Delta f$ of Eq. (26) grows to match the noise of the amplifier that follows the detector (a transimpedance amplifier, TIA, which converts current into voltage). That is roughly the best gain; beyond it, more gain lowers the SNR. Because $F$ rises with slope $k$ ($F\\approx kM+2(1-k)$ for $M\\gg1$), a material with a large $k$ reaches that point at a much lower $M$. Substituting the $k$ of each material into Eq. (25) gives:",
   "why": "F climbs from M=1, so it marks no point; the optimum is where multiplied shot noise reaches the amplifier noise."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-038",
   "finding": "report #36 (k values untagged; practical gain tagged inconsistently)",
   "old": "| Silicon APD (905/940 nm) | ≈0.02 | 2.9 at $M=50$; 4.0 at $M=100$ | ≈50–200 [U] |\n| InGaAs/InP APD (1,550 nm) | ≈0.4–0.5 | 5.5 at $M=10$; 10 at $M=20$ | ≈10–20 (§C4) |",
   "new": "| Silicon APD (905/940 nm) | ≈0.02 [U] | 2.9 at $M=50$; 4.0 at $M=100$ | ≈50–200 [U] |\n| InGaAs/InP APD (1,550 nm) | ≈0.4–0.5 [U] (§C4) | 5.5 at $M=10$; 10 at $M=20$ | ≈10–20 [U] (§C4) |",
   "why": "Same tag form in both rows; no source for k or the practical gain is cited in the report, so both are [U] (source request in skipped)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-039",
   "finding": "report #4; report #34 (SPAD expanded above); report #16",
   "old": "- **What the table means.** Silicon's small $k$ keeps $F$ near 3–4 even at a gain of 100, whereas InGaAs/InP reaches the same excess noise at a gain of about 10. A 1,550 nm linear APD receiver therefore gets far less useful gain, and needs more signal photons per point.\n- **Geiger mode (SPAD, SiPM).** A single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD) is biased above breakdown; a silicon photomultiplier (SiPM) is an array of SPAD cells whose outputs are summed. Each detected photon produces a saturated, digital avalanche with a gain of order $10^5$–$10^6$, so the count carries no analog excess noise. The costs move elsewhere: the photon detection efficiency (PDE), dead time and pile-up, afterpulsing (false counts from charge trapped in an earlier avalanche) and, in SiPMs, optical crosstalk between cells (§C4b, §C5a, §C6).\n- **Coherent receivers need no avalanche gain.** The LO supplies it: the beat signal scales with the square root of the LO power. A balanced PIN pair (two ordinary unity-gain photodiodes whose outputs are subtracted, cancelling the LO's own intensity noise) becomes shot-noise-limited once the LO's shot noise exceeds the TIA's thermal noise, which for Ge photodiodes takes about 0.3–1 mW of LO (§C4c).",
   "new": "- **What the table means.** Silicon's small $k$ keeps $F$ near 3–4 even at a gain of 100, whereas InGaAs/InP with $k=0.45$ already reaches $F=4$ at $M\\approx6.6$ and $F=5.5$ at $M=10$ [E: setting $F=4$ in Eq. (25) gives $0.45M+0.55(2-1/M)=4$, i.e. $0.45M^2-2.9M-0.55=0$, so $M=(2.9+\\sqrt{2.9^2+4\\times0.45\\times0.55})/(2\\times0.45)=6.6$]. A 1,550 nm linear APD receiver therefore gets far less useful gain, and needs more signal photons per point.\n- **Geiger mode (SPAD, SiPM).** A SPAD is biased above breakdown; a silicon photomultiplier (SiPM) is an array of SPAD cells whose outputs are summed. Each detected photon produces a saturated, digital avalanche with a gain of order $10^5$–$10^6$, so the count carries no analog excess noise. The costs move elsewhere: the photon detection efficiency (PDE), dead time and pile-up, afterpulsing (false counts from charge trapped in an earlier avalanche) and, in SiPMs, optical crosstalk between cells (§C4b, §C5a, §C6).\n- **Coherent receivers need no avalanche gain.** The LO supplies it: the beat signal scales with the square root of the LO power. A balanced pair of PIN photodiodes (ordinary p–i–n diodes with unity gain, whose outputs are subtracted to cancel the LO's own intensity noise) becomes shot-noise-limited once the LO's shot noise exceeds the TIA's input noise. With a TIA of input noise $i_{\\rm th}=3\\ {\\rm pA\\,Hz^{-1/2}}$, parity ($2qI_{\\rm LO}=i_{\\rm th}^2$) needs an LO photocurrent $I_{\\rm LO}=i_{\\rm th}^2/(2q)=(3\\times10^{-12})^2/(2\\times1.60\\times10^{-19})=28\\ \\mu{\\rm A}$, about 30 µW of LO light. A balanced Ge pair with 0.3–1 mW of LO stays at 82–94% of the shot-noise limit even at 125 °C, and Rogers et al. (2021) reached parity at 5 µW of LO per pixel with a TIA integrated on the same chip (§C4c).",
   "why": "InGaAs reaches F=4 already at M=6.6 (solved with substitution), not at M=10; the LO power is set by the TIA noise, with the §C4c numbers."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-040",
   "finding": "report #8",
   "old": "A return counts as detected when it crosses a threshold set above the noise. The threshold trades detection probability $P_D$ (the chance that a real echo crosses it) against false-alarm probability $P_{FA}$ (the chance that noise alone crosses it), and both depend on how many range cells are tested. That is why a stated range means little without its $P_D$ and false-alarm definition (§C3b). This subsection shows how many signal photons a detection needs, first for photon counting and then for coherent detection of a speckled target.\n- **Photon counting (SPAD histograms).** A SPAD receiver builds a histogram of photon arrival times over many pulses, one bin per range cell. Background counts per bin are Poisson-distributed with mean $b$. Write $P(N\\ge n\\mid\\mu)$ for the probability that a Poisson count $N$ of mean $\\mu$ is at least $n$. For a threshold of $n_{\\rm th}$ counts, the per-bin false-alarm probability is then $P(N\\ge n_{\\rm th}\\mid b)$, and across $N_{\\rm bins}$ bins in the range window the chance of a false point is about $N_{\\rm bins}$ times that. With a mean signal of $s$ counts in the echo's bin, $P_D=P(N\\ge n_{\\rm th}\\mid s+b)$. Worked cases for 1,000 bins and at most $10^{-3}$ false points per measurement, i.e. $10^{-6}$ per bin [E]:",
   "new": "A return counts as detected when it crosses a threshold set above the noise. The threshold trades detection probability $P_D$ (the chance that a real echo crosses it) against the false-alarm probability $p$ of each range cell (the chance that noise alone crosses it there). Noise can cross in any of the cells searched, so the chance of a false point, $P_{FA,{\\rm pt}}$, grows with their number, and so does the threshold needed for a given false-point rate. That is why a stated range means little without its $P_D$ and false-alarm definition (§C3b). This subsection shows how many signal photons a detection needs, first for photon counting and then for coherent detection of a speckled target.\n- **Photon counting (SPAD histograms).** A SPAD receiver builds a histogram of photon arrival times over many pulses, one bin per range cell. Background counts per bin are Poisson-distributed with mean $b$. Write $P(N\\ge n\\mid\\mu)$ for the probability that a Poisson count $N$ of mean $\\mu$ is at least $n$. For a threshold of $n_{\\rm th}$ counts, the per-bin false-alarm probability is then $p=P(N\\ge n_{\\rm th}\\mid b)$, and across $N_{\\rm bins}$ bins in the range window the chance of a false point is $P_{FA,{\\rm pt}}\\approx N_{\\rm bins}\\,p$. With a mean signal of $s$ counts in the echo's bin (the $N_s$ of subsection 1 and the $\\bar n$ of subsection 10), $P_D=P(N\\ge n_{\\rm th}\\mid s+b)$. Worked cases for $N_{\\rm bins}=1{,}000$ bins (2 ns each, over 0–300 m) and at most $P_{FA,{\\rm pt}}=10^{-3}$ false points per point, i.e. $p=10^{-6}$ per bin [E]. The daylight background $b=1$ is illustrative; §C0 subsection 10.3 uses 2,000 bins of 1 ns and the bright-sun $b=2.87$ of the §C7a model, which raises the daylight requirement from ≈13 to ≈17 counts:",
   "why": "Only the threshold depends on the number of cells; per-bin p and per-point P_FA,pt named as in §C0 subsection 10.3; the worked cases state bin width, illustrative b and how they differ from 10.3; s linked to N_s and n-bar (report #2)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-041",
   "finding": "report #8; follow-up #12",
   "old": "  - daylight at $10^{-6}$ false points per measurement: the threshold rises to 12 counts and the signal needed to ≈16. The per-bin budget becomes $10^{-6}/1{,}000=10^{-9}$; $P(N\\ge11\\mid1)=1.0\\times10^{-8}$ exceeds it and $P(N\\ge12\\mid1)=8.3\\times10^{-10}$ meets it, and $P(N\\ge12\\mid s+b)=0.9$ at $s+b\\approx16.6$, so $s\\approx16$.\n- **Why automotive thresholds are strict.** At 1.5 Mpts/s and 1,000 bins, a per-bin $P_{FA}$ of $10^{-6}$ still gives ≈1,500 false points a second, 150 per 10 Hz frame [E: $1.5\\times10^6\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\times1{,}000\\times10^{-6}=1{,}500\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}$, and $1{,}500/10=150$]. Products therefore add multi-pulse or neighbour-consistency checks before a point is output.\n- **Vendors define the terms differently.** InnovizOne's range table uses 90% true positives and a 5% false-alarm rate *per pixel* at 20 klx; Seyond's Falcon K1 uses >90% detection probability at 100 klx [P]. (klx is thousands of lux of ambient illuminance; 100 klx corresponds to bright direct sunlight.)",
   "new": "  - daylight at $P_{FA,{\\rm pt}}=10^{-6}$ false points per point: the threshold rises to 12 counts and the signal needed to ≈16. The per-bin budget becomes $p=10^{-6}/1{,}000=10^{-9}$; $P(N\\ge11\\mid1)=1.0\\times10^{-8}$ exceeds it and $P(N\\ge12\\mid1)=8.3\\times10^{-10}$ meets it, and $P(N\\ge12\\mid s+b)=0.9$ at $s+b\\approx16.6$, so $s\\approx16$.\n- **Why automotive thresholds are strict.** At 1.5 Mpts/s and 1,000 bins, a per-bin $p$ of $10^{-6}$ still gives ≈1,500 false points a second, 150 per 10 Hz frame [E: $1.5\\times10^6\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\times1{,}000\\times10^{-6}=1{,}500\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}$, and $1{,}500/10=150$]. Products therefore add multi-pulse or neighbour-consistency checks before a point is output.\n- **Vendors define the terms differently.** InnovizOne's range table uses 90% true positives and a 5% false-alarm rate *per pixel* at 20 klx [P], and InnovizTwo's datasheet 90% true and 1% false positives per pixel at 100 klx (§C3b); Seyond's Falcon K1 uses >90% detection probability at 100 klx [P]. (klx is thousands of lux of ambient illuminance; 100 klx corresponds to bright direct sunlight.)",
   "why": "Per-point and per-bin symbols as in 10.3; InnovizTwo's terms, which §C3b documents, are named next to InnovizOne's."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-042",
   "finding": "report #8; report #12",
   "old": "  $$P_D=P_{FA}^{1/(1+\\overline{\\rm SNR})} \\tag{27}$$\n\n  where $\\overline{\\rm SNR}$ is the mean signal-to-noise ratio, averaged over the speckle fading. This is the Swerling I case of radar theory. It assumes exponentially distributed intensity (Rayleigh amplitude fading) that stays constant during the look and changes independently from one look to the next.\n  - $P_D=0.9$ at $P_{FA}=10^{-6}$ then needs a mean SNR of ≈130 (21 dB). A steady, non-fading target needs ≈20 (13 dB, Albersheim's approximation) [E].\n    - *Swerling I:* taking logarithms of Eq. (27), $1+\\overline{\\rm SNR}=\\ln P_{FA}/\\ln P_D=\\ln(10^{-6})/\\ln(0.9)=(-13.82)/(-0.1054)=131$, so $\\overline{\\rm SNR}\\approx130$, and $10\\log_{10}130=21.1$ dB.\n    - *Steady target:* Albersheim's empirical approximation for a non-fading target is ${\\rm SNR_{dB}}=(6.2+4.54/\\sqrt{1.44})\\log_{10}(\\mathcal{A}+0.12\\mathcal{A}\\mathcal{B}+1.7\\mathcal{B})$, with $\\mathcal{A}=\\ln(0.62/P_{FA})=13.34$ and $\\mathcal{B}=\\ln[P_D/(1-P_D)]=\\ln9=2.20$. The constants 6.2, 4.54, 0.12, 1.7 and 0.62 are Albersheim's fitted coefficients. The general form, for $N_p$ pulses integrated incoherently, contains $\\sqrt{N_p+0.44}$, which is $\\sqrt{1.44}$ for the single pulse here. ($\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ are auxiliary numbers, not an area or a bandwidth.) That gives $9.98\\times\\log_{10}(20.6)=13.1$ dB, an SNR of ≈20.\n  - In a shot-noise-limited coherent receiver SNR ≈ detected photons, so a single look needs ≈130 photoelectrons at $10^{-6}$ per bin. A full FMCW point is held to the stricter per-point rule of §C0 subsection 10.3 (at most $10^{-3}$ false points over a 0–300 m search, with the echo's peak found in both chirp ramps). With one look it then needs ≈259 photoelectrons, twice the single-bin ≈130; four independent looks bring the total, summed over both ramps and all looks, down to ≈88. Polarization, wavelength or spatial diversity recovers much of the fading penalty. Subsection 10.3 plots these cases against photon counting (Figure C0.10b).",
   "new": "  $$P_D=p^{1/(1+\\overline{\\rm SNR})} \\tag{27}$$\n\n  where $p$ is the false-alarm probability of the bin tested and $\\overline{\\rm SNR}$ the mean signal-to-noise ratio, averaged over the speckle fading. This is the Swerling I case of radar theory. It assumes exponentially distributed intensity (Rayleigh amplitude fading) that stays constant during the look and changes independently from one look to the next.\n  - $P_D=0.9$ at $p=10^{-6}$ then needs a mean SNR of ≈130 (21 dB). A steady, non-fading target needs ≈20 (13 dB, Albersheim's approximation) [E].\n    - *Swerling I:* taking logarithms of Eq. (27), $1+\\overline{\\rm SNR}=\\ln p/\\ln P_D=\\ln(10^{-6})/\\ln(0.9)=(-13.82)/(-0.1054)=131$, so $\\overline{\\rm SNR}\\approx130$, and $10\\log_{10}130=21.1$ dB.\n    - *Steady target:* Albersheim's empirical approximation for a non-fading target is ${\\rm SNR_{dB}}=(6.2+4.54/\\sqrt{1.44})\\log_{10}(\\mathcal{A}+0.12\\mathcal{A}\\mathcal{B}+1.7\\mathcal{B})$, with $\\mathcal{A}=\\ln(0.62/p)=13.34$ and $\\mathcal{B}=\\ln[P_D/(1-P_D)]=\\ln9=2.20$. The constants 6.2, 4.54, 0.12, 1.7 and 0.62 are Albersheim's fitted coefficients. The general form, for $N_p$ pulses integrated incoherently, contains $\\sqrt{N_p+0.44}$, which is $\\sqrt{1.44}$ for the single pulse here. ($\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ are auxiliary numbers, not an area or a bandwidth.) That gives $9.98\\times\\log_{10}(20.6)=13.1$ dB, an SNR of ≈20.\n  - In a shot-noise-limited coherent receiver SNR ≈ detected photons, so a single look needs ≈130 photoelectrons at $10^{-6}$ per bin. A full FMCW point is held to the stricter per-point rule of §C0 subsection 10.3 (at most $10^{-3}$ false points over a 0–300 m search, with the echo's peak found in both chirp ramps). With one look it then needs ≈259 photoelectrons, twice the single-bin ≈130; four independent looks bring the total, summed over both ramps and all looks, down to ≈88. Polarization, wavelength or spatial diversity recovers much of the fading penalty. §C0 subsection 10.3 plots these cases against photon counting (Figure C0.10b).",
   "why": "Per-bin probability written p, as in Eq. (38) of §C0 subsection 10.3, which cites Eq. (27); §C0 prefix on subsection 10.3."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-043",
   "finding": "report #10",
   "old": "and why fog costs 905 nm and 1,550 nm about the same.",
   "new": "and why dense fog costs 905 nm and 1,550 nm about the same.",
   "why": "Wavelength independence holds for dense fog only."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-044",
   "finding": "report #30; report #10; report #36",
   "old": "- **Fog.** Kim's model scales $\\alpha$ as $(\\lambda/550\\ {\\rm nm})^{-q_{\\rm K}}$, with an exponent $q_{\\rm K}$ that describes the droplet size distribution: it falls from 1.6 in clear air to 0 below 500 m visibility. When $q_{\\rm K}=0$, $\\alpha$ no longer depends on wavelength, because fog droplets are much larger than the wavelength. Fog therefore attenuates 905 and 1,550 nm about equally [T, free-space-optics literature], consistent with the <10% difference measured by Duthon et al. (§C4c). A signal-limited lidar with 200 m clear-air range at 10% reflectance reaches the range where $R^2e^{2\\alpha R}$ equals its clear-air value [E]:",
   "new": "- **Fog.** Kim's model scales $\\alpha$ as $(\\lambda/550\\ {\\rm nm})^{-q_{\\rm K}}$, with an exponent $q_{\\rm K}$ that describes the droplet size distribution: it is 1.6 above 50 km visibility, 1.3 for 6–50 km (the \"clear air\" of subsection 10.1), and falls to 0 below 500 m visibility. When $q_{\\rm K}=0$, $\\alpha$ no longer depends on wavelength, because fog droplets are much larger than the wavelength. Dense fog (visibility below 500 m, where $q_{\\rm K}=0$) therefore attenuates 905 and 1,550 nm about equally [I, Kim, McArthur and Korevaar 2001], consistent with the <10% difference measured by Duthon et al. (§C4c). In light fog the model still favours 1,550 nm slightly: at 1 km visibility $\\alpha=3.05$ /km at 905 nm against 2.33 /km at 1,550 nm. A signal-limited lidar with 200 m clear-air range at 10% reflectance reaches the range where $R^2e^{2\\alpha R}$ equals its clear-air value [E]:",
   "why": "Kim's exponent per visibility band (1.3 is the clear air of 10.1), the equal-attenuation claim limited to dense fog, the light-fog difference given, and an [I] tag for the peer-reviewed model source (listed in the sources of §C0 subsection 10.9)."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-045",
   "finding": "report #10; report #37",
   "old": "| Visibility $V$ | $\\alpha$ | Two-way loss at 50 m | Reach of a 200 m (clear-air) lidar | Reach / $V$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 m (light fog; $q_{\\rm K}=0.5$) | 3.0 /km | 1.3 dB | ≈133 m | 0.13 |\n| 200 m (fog) | 19.6 /km | 8.5 dB | ≈61 m | 0.30 |\n| 50 m (dense fog) | 78 /km | 34 dB | ≈26 m | 0.52 |\n| 20 m (very dense fog) | 196 /km | 85 dB | ≈14 m | 0.69 |",
   "new": "| Visibility $V$ | $\\alpha$ at 905 nm | Two-way loss at 50 m | Reach of a 200 m (clear-air) lidar | Reach / $V$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 m (light fog; $q_{\\rm K}=0.5$) | 3.0 /km | 1.3 dB | ≈133 m | 0.13 |\n| 200 m (fog) | 19.6 /km | 8.5 dB | ≈61 m | 0.30 |\n| 50 m (dense fog) | 78 /km | 34 dB | ≈26 m | 0.52 |\n| 20 m (very dense fog) | 196 /km | 85 dB | ≈13.7 m | 0.69 |",
   "why": "The 1 km row is computed at 905 nm; the 20 m reach shown to the precision its ratio uses."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-046",
   "finding": "report #10; report #35 (/km); report #37",
   "old": "  - **Extinction:** Kim's model gives $\\alpha=(3.912/V)\\,(\\lambda/550\\ {\\rm nm})^{-q_{\\rm K}}$, evaluated here at 905 nm. Between 0.5 and 1 km visibility the model sets $q_{\\rm K}=V-0.5$ (with $V$ in km), which is 0.5 at 1 km. For $V=1{,}000$ m with $q_{\\rm K}=0.5$, $\\alpha=3.912\\ {\\rm km^{-1}}\\times(905/550)^{-0.5}=3.912\\times0.78=3.05$ /km. Below 500 m visibility $q_{\\rm K}=0$, so $\\alpha=3.912/V$: 19.6 /km at 200 m, 78 /km at 50 m and 196 /km at 20 m.\n  - **Two-way loss at 50 m:** $10\\log_{10}e^{2\\alpha R}=4.343\\times2\\alpha R$ dB, where $4.343=10/\\ln10=10\\log_{10}e$ converts natural-log units into decibels. For 200 m visibility this is $4.343\\times2\\times0.0196\\ {\\rm m^{-1}}\\times50\\ {\\rm m}=8.5$ dB; the other rows scale with $\\alpha$ (1.3, 34 and 85 dB).\n  - **Reach:** a signal-limited lidar still detects while $e^{-2\\alpha R}/R^2$ is at least its clear-air value at 200 m, $1/(200\\ {\\rm m})^2$. The reach is therefore the root of $R^2e^{2\\alpha R}=(200\\ {\\rm m})^2$, found numerically. For 200 m visibility, $R=61$ m gives $61^2\\times e^{2\\times0.0196\\times61}=3{,}721\\times10.9\\approx4.0\\times10^4$ m², as required.",
   "new": "  - **Extinction:** Kim's model gives $\\alpha=(3.912/V)\\,(\\lambda/550\\ {\\rm nm})^{-q_{\\rm K}}$, evaluated here at 905 nm. Between 0.5 and 1 km visibility the model sets $q_{\\rm K}=V-0.5$ (with $V$ in km), which is 0.5 at 1 km. For $V=1{,}000$ m with $q_{\\rm K}=0.5$, $\\alpha=(3.912/{\\rm km})\\times(905/550)^{-0.5}=3.912\\times0.78=3.05$ /km at 905 nm, and $3.912\\times(1{,}550/550)^{-0.5}=3.912\\times0.596=2.33$ /km at 1,550 nm. Below 500 m visibility $q_{\\rm K}=0$, so $\\alpha=3.912/V$: 19.6 /km at 200 m, 78 /km at 50 m and 196 /km at 20 m.\n  - **Two-way loss at 50 m:** $10\\log_{10}e^{2\\alpha R}=4.343\\times2\\alpha R$ dB, where $4.343=10/\\ln10=10\\log_{10}e$ converts natural-log units into decibels. For 200 m visibility this is $4.343\\times2\\times0.0196\\ {\\rm m^{-1}}\\times50\\ {\\rm m}=8.5$ dB; the other rows scale with $\\alpha$ (1.3, 34 and 85 dB).\n  - **Reach:** a signal-limited lidar still detects while $e^{-2\\alpha R}/R^2$ is at least its clear-air value at 200 m, $1/(200\\ {\\rm m})^2$. The reach is therefore the root of $R^2e^{2\\alpha R}=(200\\ {\\rm m})^2$, found numerically. For 200 m visibility, $R=61$ m gives $61^2\\times e^{2\\times0.0196\\times61}=3{,}721\\times10.9=4.06\\times10^4$ m², within 1.5% of $(200\\ {\\rm m})^2=4.0\\times10^4$ m², as required.",
   "why": "Adds the 1,550 nm light-fog value with substitution, one unit form, and the exact check value."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-047",
   "finding": "report #9",
   "old": "- **Reach relative to visibility rises as fog thickens,** so the \"≈0.6–1 V\" of §E4 describes the dense end. The reason is that at a shorter reach the $1/R^2$ spreading loss is smaller, so the lidar can afford a larger fog optical depth $\\alpha R$; and below 500 m visibility, where $\\alpha=3.912/V$, reach divided by visibility is just that optical depth divided by 3.912.",
   "new": "- **Reach relative to visibility rises as fog thickens:** about 0.5–0.7 V for visibilities of 20–50 m in this model. The \"≈0.6–1 V\" of §E4 therefore applies only below about 30 m visibility, and reach equals V only near 4 m visibility, where $\\alpha R=3.912$ [E: with $q_{\\rm K}=0$ the reach solves $R^2e^{2\\alpha R}=(200\\ {\\rm m})^2$, i.e. $R=200\\ {\\rm m}\\times e^{-\\alpha R}$; a reach of 0.6 V means $\\alpha R=0.6\\times3.912=2.35$, so $R=200\\ {\\rm m}\\times e^{-2.35}=19$ m and $V=19/0.6=32$ m, while a reach of 1 V gives $R=200\\ {\\rm m}\\times e^{-3.912}=4.0$ m]. The ratio rises because at a shorter reach the $1/R^2$ spreading loss is smaller, so the lidar can afford a larger fog optical depth $\\alpha R$. Below 500 m visibility, where $\\alpha=3.912/V$, reach divided by visibility is that optical depth divided by 3.912.",
   "why": "The model gives 0.5-0.7 V at 20-50 m visibility, so §E4's 0.6-1 V holds only below about 30 m; the long sentence is split and the claim derived."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-048",
   "finding": "report #36 (rain tag); report #34 (ITU-R); consistency flag (rain: §C4c reports a 2x difference)",
   "old": "- **Rain.** The empirical free-space-optics law $\\gamma_{\\rm rain}\\approx1.076\\,R_r^{0.67}$ dB/km gives the specific attenuation $\\gamma_{\\rm rain}$, the loss per kilometre of path, for a rain rate $R_r$ in mm/h; 1.076 and 0.67 are Carbonneau's fitted coefficients, as used with ITU-R P.1814. The law yields 3.2, 9.3 and 23.5 dB/km at 5, 25 and 100 mm/h (for example, $1.076\\times5^{0.67}=1.076\\times2.94=3.2$ dB/km). At 200 m range the light travels 0.4 km out and back, so the loss is only $3.2\\times0.4=1.3$, $9.3\\times0.4=3.7$ and $23.5\\times0.4=9.4$ dB [T/E]. Raindrops are far larger than the wavelength, so this loss barely depends on it. In practice rain hurts more through:",
   "new": "- **Rain.** The empirical free-space-optics law $\\gamma_{\\rm rain}\\approx1.076\\,R_r^{0.67}$ dB/km gives the specific attenuation $\\gamma_{\\rm rain}$, the loss per kilometre of path, for a rain rate $R_r$ in mm/h; 1.076 and 0.67 are Carbonneau's fitted coefficients, as used with ITU-R P.1814, a recommendation of the International Telecommunication Union's radiocommunication sector (ITU-R). The law yields 3.2, 9.3 and 23.5 dB/km at 5, 25 and 100 mm/h (for example, $1.076\\times5^{0.67}=1.076\\times2.94=3.2$ dB/km). At 200 m range the light travels 0.4 km out and back, so the loss is only $3.2\\times0.4=1.3$, $9.3\\times0.4=3.7$ and $23.5\\times0.4=9.4$ dB [P, ITU-R P.1814; E for the 200 m losses]. Raindrops are far larger than the wavelength, so in this law the loss barely depends on it; one comparison cited in §C4c nevertheless found 905 nm reaching ≈2× farther than 1,550 nm at 25 mm/h [I, Wojtanowski 2014 via survey]. In practice rain hurts more through:",
   "why": "Expands ITU-R, tags the law to the recommendation with [E] for the 200 m losses, and points to the §C4c comparison that shows a wavelength difference."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-049",
   "finding": "report #24",
   "old": "lidar 3D detection fell from 52.3 to 29.7 AP (average precision, a 0–100 score of object-detection accuracy) while radar held 44.5 (§E4).",
   "new": "lidar-only 3D detection fell from 52.3 to 29.7 AP (average precision, a 0–100 score of object-detection accuracy), while radar-only detection fell only from 49.9 to 44.5 [I, K-Radar, NeurIPS 2022; §E4].",
   "why": "Radar fell from 49.9 to 44.5 (§E4), it did not hold; evidence tag added for the K-Radar paper (NeurIPS 2022, in the report's sources)."
  },
  {
