  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-055",
   "finding": "report #39",
   "old": "- **Ambiguity range:** $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2f_m)$, e.g. $3.0\\times10^8/(2\\times100\\times10^6)=1.5$ m at 100 MHz; the same gives 0.3 m at 500 MHz, 7.5 m at 20 MHz and 50 m at 3 MHz.\n- **Range per radian:** $c/(4\\pi f_m)$, e.g. $3.0\\times10^8/(4\\pi\\times100\\times10^6)=0.24$ m/rad at 100 MHz, and likewise 0.048, 1.2 and 8.0 m/rad at 500, 20 and 3 MHz.\n- **Precision:** $\\sigma_R=[c/(4\\pi f_m)]\\,\\sigma_\\varphi$ with an assumed phase noise of $\\sigma_\\varphi=0.02$ rad, e.g. $0.24\\ {\\rm m/rad}\\times0.02\\ {\\rm rad}=4.8$ mm ≈ 5 mm at 100 MHz; the other rows give $0.048\\times0.02=0.96$ mm, $1.2\\times0.02=2.4$ cm and $8.0\\times0.02=0.16$ m.\n- **Z+F's lowest frequency:** setting $R_{\\rm amb}=365$ m in $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2f_m)$ gives $f_m=3.0\\times10^8/(2\\times365)\\ {\\rm Hz}=0.41$ MHz, and $c/(4\\pi f_m)=R_{\\rm amb}/(2\\pi)=365/(2\\pi)=58$ m/rad.",
   "new": "- **Unambiguous range:** $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2f_m)$, e.g. $3.0\\times10^8/(2\\times100\\times10^6)=1.5$ m at 100 MHz; the same gives 0.3 m at 500 MHz, 7.5 m at 20 MHz and 50 m at 3 MHz.\n- **Range per radian:** $c/(4\\pi f_m)$, e.g. $3.0\\times10^8/(4\\pi\\times100\\times10^6)=0.24$ m/rad at 100 MHz, and likewise 0.048, 1.2 and 8.0 m/rad at 500, 20 and 3 MHz.\n- **Precision:** $\\sigma_R=[c/(4\\pi f_m)]\\,\\sigma_\\varphi$ with an assumed phase noise of $\\sigma_\\varphi=0.02$ rad, e.g. $0.24\\ {\\rm m/rad}\\times0.02\\ {\\rm rad}=4.8$ mm ≈ 5 mm at 100 MHz; the other rows give $0.048\\times0.02=0.96$ mm, $1.2\\times0.02=2.4$ cm and $8.0\\times0.02=0.16$ m.\n- **Z+F's lowest frequency:** Z+F's datasheet calls its 365 m unambiguous range the \"ambiguity interval\". Setting $R_{\\rm amb}=365$ m in $R_{\\rm amb}=c/(2f_m)$ gives $f_m=3.0\\times10^8/(2\\times365)\\ {\\rm Hz}=0.41$ MHz, and $c/(4\\pi f_m)=R_{\\rm amb}/(2\\pi)=365/(2\\pi)=58$ m/rad.",
   "why": "One name for R_amb; Z+F's own term kept once, attributed."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-056",
   "finding": "report #32; report #36 (Wikipedia is [S]); report #34 (AGA)",
   "old": "**One principle, three kinds of product.** Phase ranging is the oldest optical ranging method.\n- **Geodimeter.** Erik Bergstrand invented it in 1947 to measure the speed of light: a Kerr-cell shutter chopped a light beam at megahertz rates. AGA commercialized it in 1953 [T, Wikipedia]. A baseline measurement gave $c = 299{,}793.1 \\pm 0.2$ km/s [S].\n- **Surveying and building trades today.** Electronic distance meters, handheld rangefinders and phase-based terrestrial laser scanners all still use it.",
   "new": "**One principle, three kinds of product.** Phase ranging was the first method of electronic distance measurement [S].\n- **Geodimeter.** Erik Bergstrand developed the Geodimeter in 1947 to measure the speed of light: a Kerr-cell shutter chopped a light beam at megahertz rates. The Swedish company AGA (AB Gasaccumulator) commercialized it in 1953 [S, Wikipedia]. A baseline measurement gave $c = 299{,}793.1 \\pm 0.2$ km/s [S].\n- **Surveying and building trades today.** Electronic distance meters, handheld rangefinders and phase-based terrestrial laser scanners all still use it.\n\nThe table compares the three kinds of product that use phase ranging today: surveying rangefinders (electronic distance meters, EDMs), phase-based laser scanners and iToF cameras.",
   "why": "Triangulation is older, so the claim is narrowed to electronic distance measurement; 'invented it' made unambiguous; the table gets a lead-in sentence."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-057",
   "finding": "report #33; report #39; report #35 (Mpts/s)",
   "old": "| **Modulation** | Leica: \"200 million waves\" for millimetres, lower frequencies for metres [S]. Mekometer: 460–510 MHz on a 633 nm HeNe laser, tuned to a phase null [S] | Several frequencies; 365 m ambiguity interval (Z+F) [P] | About 10–200 MHz: MLX75023 up to 40 MHz; ST VD55H1 200 MHz at >85% contrast [T] |\n| **Time per point** | Roughly 0.1–1 s [U] | ≈1–7 µs: up to 1.094 M pts/s, noise specified at 136,719 pts/s (Z+F) [P] | Every pixel at once, over a frame exposure of ms |",
   "new": "| **Modulation** | Leica: a fine-ranging frequency its FAQ calls \"200 million waves\" (per second, i.e. about 200 MHz [U]) for millimetres, lower frequencies for metres [S]. Mekometer: 460–510 MHz on a 633 nm HeNe laser, tuned to a phase null [S] | Several frequencies; 365 m unambiguous range (Z+F) [P] | About 10–200 MHz: MLX75023 up to 40 MHz; ST VD55H1 200 MHz at >85% contrast [T] |\n| **Time per point** | Roughly 0.1–1 s [U] | ≈1–7 µs: up to 1.094 Mpts/s, noise specified at 136,719 pts/s (Z+F) [P] | Every pixel at once, over a frame exposure of ms |",
   "why": "Says what '200 million waves' means, uses one name for the unambiguous range and one unit form."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-058",
   "finding": "report #18",
   "old": "Why can one scanner reach 365 m with millimetre noise while a ToF camera stops near 10 m?",
   "new": "Why can a phase scanner range to 365 m, with 0.3–3.7 mm noise at 50–100 m, while a ToF camera stops near 10 m?",
   "why": "365 m is the Z+F's unambiguous range; its millimetre noise is specified at 50-100 m."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-059",
   "finding": "report #27; symbol scheme (E_pt)",
   "old": "1. **Ambient light per unit signal.** Take a scanner at 10 mW average that dwells 7.3 µs per point (the inverse of Z+F's 136,719 pts/s) with a matched field of view of 0.6 mrad (full angle, twice the Z+F beam's 0.3 mrad half-angle, so $\\Omega=\\pi\\times(0.3\\times10^{-3})^2\\approx2.8\\times10^{-7}$ sr): that is $10^{-2}\\ {\\rm W}\\times7.3\\times10^{-6}\\ {\\rm s}=73$ nJ per point. Take a VGA camera flooding 70° × 55° with 1 W for a 10 ms exposure: that is $1\\ {\\rm W}\\times0.01\\ {\\rm s}/307{,}200=33$ nJ per pixel, with $\\Omega\\approx(1.222\\times0.960)/307{,}200\\approx3.8\\times10^{-6}$ sr per pixel. Here 70° and 55° are 1.222 and 0.960 rad; the exact solid angle of a 70° × 55° rectangular field, 1.07 sr rather than 1.17 sr, would give $3.5\\times10^{-6}$ sr and lower the ratio below by about 9%.\n   - The laser energy per point is similar.\n   - Ambient light collected per unit signal scales as $\\Omega\\,T_{\\rm exp}/E_{\\rm point}$. Here $\\Omega$ is the solid angle one detector or pixel sees (sr), $T_{\\rm exp}$ the time it integrates light for that point (the dwell of the scanner or the exposure of the camera; s), and $E_{\\rm point}$ the laser energy spent on that point (J). That is $3.8\\times10^{-6}\\times0.01/(33\\times10^{-9})\\approx1.2$ sr·s/J for the camera against $2.8\\times10^{-7}\\times7.3\\times10^{-6}/(73\\times10^{-9})\\approx2.8\\times10^{-5}$ for the scanner, so the camera collects about **$4\\times10^4$ times more sunlight per unit signal** ($1.17/(2.83\\times10^{-5})\\approx4.1\\times10^4$).",
   "new": "1. **Ambient light per unit signal.** Let $\\Omega$ be the solid angle one detector or pixel sees (sr). Take a scanner at 10 mW average power that dwells 7.3 µs per point, the inverse of Z+F's 136,719 pts/s: $10^{-2}\\ {\\rm W}\\times7.3\\times10^{-6}\\ {\\rm s}=73$ nJ per point. Its detector sees a matched 0.6 mrad full angle, twice the Z+F beam's 0.3 mrad half-angle, so $\\Omega=\\pi\\times(0.3\\times10^{-3})^2\\approx2.8\\times10^{-7}$ sr. Take a VGA camera flooding 70° × 55° with 1 W for a 10 ms exposure: $1\\ {\\rm W}\\times0.01\\ {\\rm s}/307{,}200=33$ nJ per pixel, with $\\Omega\\approx(1.222\\times0.960)/307{,}200\\approx3.8\\times10^{-6}$ sr per pixel. Here 70° and 55° are 1.222 and 0.960 rad, and their product, 1.17 sr, approximates the field's solid angle; the exact solid angle of a 70° × 55° rectangular field, 1.07 sr, would give $3.5\\times10^{-6}$ sr and lower the ratio below by about 9%.\n   - The laser energy per point is similar.\n   - Ambient light collected per unit signal scales as $\\Omega\\,T_{\\rm exp}/E_{\\rm pt}$, where $T_{\\rm exp}$ is the time the detector integrates light for that point (the dwell of the scanner or the exposure of the camera; s) and $E_{\\rm pt}$ the laser energy spent on that point (J). That is $3.8\\times10^{-6}\\times0.01/(33\\times10^{-9})\\approx1.2$ sr·s/J for the camera against $2.8\\times10^{-7}\\times7.3\\times10^{-6}/(73\\times10^{-9})\\approx2.8\\times10^{-5}$ sr·s/J for the scanner. $T_{\\rm exp}$ cancels, because $E_{\\rm pt}$ is the laser power times $T_{\\rm exp}$ (shared among the pixels, for the camera), so each figure is the solid angle over which the laser's power is spread, divided by that power: $1.17\\ {\\rm sr}/1\\ {\\rm W}=1.17$ sr·s/J for the camera and $2.83\\times10^{-7}\\ {\\rm sr}/0.01\\ {\\rm W}=2.83\\times10^{-5}$ sr·s/J for the scanner. The camera therefore collects about **$4\\times10^4$ times more sunlight per unit signal** ($1.17/(2.83\\times10^{-5})\\approx4.1\\times10^4$).",
   "why": "Splits the 51-word sentence, defines Omega before use, gives both figures one unit, and shows why T_exp cancels."
  },
  {
   "id": "02_C0_parts1-9-060",
   "finding": "report #21",
   "old": "**Where long range goes.** Pulsed dToF (subsection 1) gates out ambient light the way a scanner's short dwell does. Its precision is set by pulse width and jitter rather than range, and a histogram separates multiple returns.",
   "new": "**Why long-range sensing uses pulses.** Pulsed dToF (subsection 1) gates out ambient light the way a scanner's short dwell does. Its precision is set by the pulse width and timing jitter, averaged over the detected photons, rather than by a modulation frequency that also fixes the unambiguous range. A histogram also separates multiple returns.",
   "why": "Precision does depend on range through N_s; the intended contrast is with a modulation frequency that also fixes the unambiguous range. Clearer lead-in."
  },
